 ------------------------------------------------------
   CSS LIST-STYLE-TYPE — DETAILED GUIDE
---------------------------------------------------------

1. What is list-style-type?
   - The CSS list-style-type property defines the marker (bullet or number) style for list items (<ul> or <ol>).
   - It lets you change how the default bullets or numbers appear.

---------------------------------------------------------
2. Common Values:

   disc                  → Solid circle (default for <ul>) •
   circle                → Hollow circle ○
   square                → Square ▪
   decimal               → Numbers 1, 2, 3 ...
   decimal-leading-zero  → Numbers with leading zeros 01, 02, 03 ...
   lower-roman           → Roman numerals lowercase i, ii, iii
   upper-roman           → Roman numerals uppercase I, II, III
   lower-alpha           → Lowercase letters a, b, c
   upper-alpha           → Uppercase letters A, B, C
   none                  → No marker

---------------------------------------------------------
3. Examples:

   /* Solid bullets (default) */
   ul { list-style-type: disc; }

   /* Hollow circles */
   ul { list-style-type: circle; }

   /* Square bullets */
   ul { list-style-type: square; }

   /* Numbered list */
   ol { list-style-type: decimal; }

   /* Roman numerals */
   ol { list-style-type: upper-roman; }

   /* Remove bullets or numbers */
   ul, ol { list-style-type: none; }

---------------------------------------------------------
4. Tips:
   - Use descriptive class names when styling lists, e.g., .menu-list, .feature-list
   - Reset list-style-type to none for custom menus
   - Works with <ul> and <ol> elements
